The tricky part: within a column, multiple letters may be unassigned. The standard approach: recursively assign the unassigned letters in the column, and after all are assigned, check whether (sum + carry) % 10 matches the digit of the result letter (assigning it if unassigned), and carry over (sum + carry) / 10.

A cleaner formulation: process the column, and for each unassigned letter in the column (including the result letter), try each free digit. After assigning all letters in the column, check the column equation:

sum + carry ≡ resDigit (mod 10), and newCarry = (sum + carry) / 10.

Actually, the simplest way to implement it is: collect the multiset of unassigned letters in this column (addends + result). Recursively assign digits to them. Once all are assigned, verify the equation.

